# cc-memory's OWN state directory is a runtime artifact, not source.
# Anyone who clones this repo will generate their own when they enable the
# plugin. Don't commit the maintainer's local memory data.
#
# ANCHORED (leading slash) ON PURPOSE. Unanchored, `.ccm/` matches at every
# depth, and a stray in-repo test project -- whose only content IS its own
# state directory -- becomes 100% invisible to `git status`. That is exactly
# how an `x/` scratch project created by a v2.5.x verification harness (0
# memories, 0 sessions, one SessionStart injection) sat in the repo root
# undetected: the tempfile-only test rule in CLAUDE.md was broken and
# .gitignore hid the evidence. This repo has exactly ONE legitimate state
# directory -- this one. Any other is a leaked test artifact and MUST show up
# as untracked.
#
# This rule keeps the PRE-v2.13.0 name. The directory is `.ccm/` since
# v2.13.0, and a clone made before the first post-upgrade session still
# carries `memory/` until a hook migrates it, so both are ignored -- but the
# `.ccm/` half CANNOT live here. It is a dotted directory, so it is governed
# by the `.*/` blanket further down, and git applies the LAST matching
# pattern: anything written here would be overruled by that blanket. Its rules
# are in that block, and they reproduce this one's anchoring exactly.
/memory/

# Per-user local Claude Code state — settings, agents, project skills.
# The PLUGIN itself ships its hooks/skills/commands/agents from the repo
# root (hooks.json, skills/, commands/, agents/), not from .claude/. So
# everything under .claude/ is the maintainer's local Claude Code config
# (cc-enforcer enforcement, personal skill toolkits, etc.) and stays local.
.claude/

.ce/

# Dotted directories are per-user tool state by default, so ignore them
# wholesale — BUT re-include the two that ARE the product. `.*/` on its own
# swallowed both, and did it silently: `.github/` went to 0 tracked files and
# vanished from `git status` entirely (the release-gate CI would simply never
# have been committed), and every FUTURE file under `.claude-plugin/` — the
# manifest that makes this a Claude Code plugin at all — would have been
# dropped the same way. The two existing manifest files survived only because
# they were already tracked, which is the worst kind of survival: it looks
# fine until someone adds the third file.
#
# Same lesson as the /memory/ block above, one pattern later: an unanchored
# blanket hides its own damage. A negation only works because git can still
# DESCEND into a re-included directory — `!.github/` re-includes the directory
# itself, not just its contents, and that ordering is load-bearing.
#
# `.ccm/` is the THIRD re-include and the subtlest. v2.13.0 renamed the state
# directory from `memory/` to `.ccm/`, which moved it under this blanket — and
# a blanket-ignored state directory is exactly the invisibility the /memory/
# block above was anchored to prevent, reintroduced by the rename. The two
# lines below restore that block's semantics for the new name, in this order
# because git applies the LAST matching pattern:
#
#   !.ccm/   un-ignores the name at EVERY depth, overriding `.*/`
#   /.ccm/   re-ignores ONLY the repo's own, at the root
#
# So this repo's own state directory stays ignored, and a stray `.ccm/` under
# a leaked in-repo test project shows up as untracked — which is the whole
# point of anchoring, and the reason the pair cannot be written up beside
# /memory/ where it would be overruled by `.*/` below it.
.*/
!.github/
!.claude-plugin/
!.ccm/
/.ccm/

# cc-tree audit output — working evidence for the v2.8.0 audit rounds
# (adjudications, raw candidate JSON, the convergence report). Excluded by
# user ruling (2026-08-09): the release notes carry the narrative inline,
# and no tracked doc cites these files, so the citation gate never points
# here. Anchored like /memory/ above, and for the same reason.
/code-audit-out/